;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/take-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "take!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(take! lst k) -> list"
  :summary "The first k elements of lst, taken by cutting the spine at k rather than by copying it. The argument is destroyed."
  :explanation "take! is the linear-update twin of take. take builds a fresh k-element list and leaves its argument untouched; take! reaches into the argument, severs the cdr of the k-th cell, and hands back the head cell it already had. The suite pins this exactly: after (take! x 3) on a six-element x, the result is eq? to x and shares every cell down to the cut. So the shortening is not a view of x, it IS x. The bang is a licence to destroy. After the call the cells that made up the argument have been re-used, so the variable you passed in no longer names what it named. Bind the result and read only that. The reason the verb exists is that take on a long list allocates k cells you are about to throw away; take! allocates none. The reason it is dangerous is that the saving is paid for out of the caller's variable, and nothing in the call site says so."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define kept (take! xs 3))\n(display (list 'returned kept))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; the destroyed binding: xs is not six long any more.\n(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define kept (take! xs 3))\n(display (list 'xs-now xs 'same-object (eq? kept xs)))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; take copies, take! cuts. Compare what happens to the source.\n(define a (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define b (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define pure (take a 3))\n(define destructive (take! b 3))\n(display (list 'a-after a 'b-after b))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The argument is destroyed. list.sld:308-313 asserts (eq? x y) and (eq? (cddr x) (cddr y)): the result is the argument, cut short."
             "The dropped tail is not returned to you. If you wanted both halves, split-at! is the verb that hands back both."
             "k must not exceed the length of lst; there is no cell at which to cut."
             "Nothing at the call site distinguishes take! from take. Reviewers cannot see the destruction; only the bang can warn them.")
  :drawbacks ("It is only a saving when the argument is genuinely dead. If any other binding still points into the list you have corrupted it at a distance."
             "The verb cannot be used on a literal or on a list you did not build, because you do not know who else holds it."
             "Reasoning about a function that calls take! stops being local: the caller has to be read too.")
  :usecases ("Trimming a large freshly-built list to length inside a producer, where the full list has no other owner."
             "Bounded queues and windows, where the tail past k is provably garbage."
             "Hot loops where the allocation of take shows up in a measurement, and only then.")
  :related ("take"
             "drop"
             "split-at!"
             "drop-right!"
             "take-while!"
             "list-tail")
  :learn (:concept "Linear update: buying a saving with the caller's data"
          :prerequisites ("take" "cdr" "set-cdr!" "eq?")
          :progression "Learn take first and use it. Reach for take! only when you can name the owner of the list and prove it is you.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 linear-update procedures; conformance evidence scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:308-313; signature derived from the take record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat. Drafted 2026-08-03 against the incoming cons-cell representation; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define kept (take! xs 3))\n(display (list 'kept kept 'xs xs))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define a (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(define b (list 1 2 3 4 5))\n(display (equal? (take a 2) (take! b 2)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4))\n(define kept (take! xs 2))\n(display (list 'result-is-argument (eq? kept xs)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator has just built a long list inside a producer and wants only its first k elements. Candidates: take (copies k cells, leaves the source intact, safe to hand anyone), list-head (not in this dialect), or take! (allocates nothing but destroys the source). The question the operator must answer first is not which is faster, it is who else holds the list. If the answer is anyone at all, take is the only correct choice."
     :explanation "eng: take! returns the prefix and leaves the caller holding a shortened list under the old name. math: as a value, take! agrees with take on every proper list and every legal k; they differ only in what they leave behind. cs: the result is eq? to the argument, which is the operational statement of \"the argument was destroyed\"."
     :meta "Choosing between a pure verb and its linear-update twin is an ownership question wearing a performance costume. Answer the ownership question first.")
    (:eng "Only spend the caller's data when the caller is you."
     :math "take! and take are the same function of the argument; they differ in what survives the call."
     :cs "Aliasing is the whole story: eq? to the argument means every other holder of that list has been changed without being told.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Shortening a sequence by cutting it where it stands, rather than by copying the part you want."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(display (take! xs 3))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a rope cut to length" :code "(define rope (list 'm1 'm2 'm3 'm4 'm5))\n(display (list 'cut (take! rope 2)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a bounded log" :code "(define (cap! entries n)\n  (if (> (length entries) n) (take! entries n) entries))\n(display (cap! (list 'a 'b 'c 'd) 2))\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, take! severs the spine at k. Cutting a rope gives you the piece you wanted and destroys the rope you had; nobody expects the offcut to still be attached. A bounded log does the same to history: the entries past the cap are not archived, they are gone. All three share the shape of a cut that consumes what it cuts."
     :meta "The shape transfers anywhere the source is single-owner and the discarded part has no claimant.")
    (:eng "A cut is cheap and irreversible; a copy is expensive and safe. Pick on ownership."
     :math "Truncation is a prefix operation; it is the copy or the cut that differs, not the prefix."
     :cs "This is the same trade as an in-place array resize versus a slice: identical results, different survivors.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define kept (take! xs 3))\n(display (list 'length (length kept) 'eq-to-arg (eq? kept xs)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (agrees? n k)\n  (let ((a (build-list-1-to n)) (b (build-list-1-to n)))\n    (equal? (take a k) (take! b k))))\n(define (build-list-1-to n)\n  (let loop ((i n) (acc (list))) (if (= i 0) acc (loop (- i 1) (cons i acc)))))\n(display (list (agrees? 6 3) (agrees? 4 0) (agrees? 4 4)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))\n(define alias xs)\n(define kept (take! xs 3))\n(display (list 'alias-was-changed-too (eq? alias kept)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "For a proper list lst and 0 <= k <= (length lst), take! returns a list equal? to (take lst k), and that list is eq? to lst itself when k > 0. Every binding that pointed at lst before the call now points at the truncated result."
     :invariant "forall proper lst, 0 <= k <= |lst|. equal?(take!(lst,k), take(lst,k)) and (k > 0 implies eq?(take!(lst,k), lst))"
     :meta "The pattern is value-agreement with survivor-disagreement. What would falsify the claim: a result not equal? to take, or a fresh list returned instead of the argument's own head cell -- which is precisely what the current array-backed implementation does, and why this record carries :status needs-alfred.")
    (:eng "The alias line is the one to read: a second name for the list was changed by a call it does not appear in."
     :math "Agreement on values is not agreement on effects; a proof of the first says nothing about the second."
     :cs "A test that only checks equal? cannot tell take from take!. That is exactly how the aliasing shipped unnoticed.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (first-page! rows n)\n  (take! rows n))\n(display (first-page! (list 'a 'b 'c 'd 'e) 2))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (halves! xs k)\n  (let ((tail (drop xs k)))\n    (list (take! xs k) tail)))\n(display (halves! (list 1 2 3 4 5) 2))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (safe-take xs k)\n  (take (list-copy xs) k))\n(display (safe-take (list 1 2 3 4) 2))\n(newline)"
     :base "take! shortens a list by cutting its spine and returns the head cell it already had."
     :emergent "eng: paging over a list you own, with no allocation per page. math: taking the tail BEFORE the cut is what makes a two-way split work at all -- once take! has run, the tail is unreachable, which is the whole argument for split-at!. cs: the defensive form -- copy, then take purely -- is what you write when you cannot prove ownership, and it costs exactly what take! was trying to save."
     :meta "The composition family here is order-of-operations under destruction: with a pure verb the order of reads does not matter, with take! it decides whether the second read is possible.")
    (:eng "Sequence matters once a verb destroys. Write the reads before the cut."
     :math "Under mutation, expressions stop commuting; the cut is a fence the other reads must be on the correct side of."
     :cs "split-at! exists because the hand-rolled take!-then-drop pairing is unwriteable in the correct order.")
  )
)
